# Wire

Typed reading of JSON documents, on top of the standard library's `json.lg`.

The factory's own records travel through the codecs LOGOS DERIVES from their
`## A … has:` blocks (`toJson_Problem`, `fromJson_Problem`, …). Two kinds of
document cannot: a record holding a `Seq` field (the derive encodes one but writes
no decoder for it — see `LOGOS_ISSUES.md`), and the foreign documents the factory
ingests (the OPDP atlas, the formalization-lab manifest, OpenRouter replies, the
kernel's `--json` reports). Both are read here, and they are read the same way:

1. the SHAPE is checked once, all of it — every required field present with the
   type it must have, and no field nobody declared — and the first violation is
   the error;
2. the fields are then read by total accessors that cannot fail, because step 1
   already established what they would have failed on.

Checking first and reading second keeps a decoder a flat list of fields instead
of a staircase of nested `Inspect`s, and it means a document is never half-read:
either every field was what the contract says, or nothing was taken from it.

## Note
A field specification is `name:kind`. The kinds are the JSON shapes the factory
reads: `text`, `int`, `bool`, `texts` (an array of strings), `object`, `array`,
`opttext` and `optint` (the value or `null`), and `any` (present, any value).

## To fieldKind (spec: Text) -> Text:
    Let parts be split(spec, ":").
    If length of parts is at least 2:
        Return item 2 of parts.
    Return "any".

## Note
The field name of a `name:kind` specification.

## To fieldName (spec: Text) -> Text:
    Let parts be split(spec, ":").
    Return item 1 of parts.

## Note
Whether every element of a JSON array is a string.

## To allStrings (elems: Seq of Json) -> Bool:
    Repeat for e in elems:
        Inspect e:
            When JStr (s):
                Let ignored be s.
            Otherwise:
                Return false.
    Return true.

## Note
Whether a JSON value has the shape a kind names. Unknown kinds match nothing, so a
mistyped specification fails loudly instead of accepting everything.

## To hasKind (v: Json, kind: Text) -> Bool:
    If kind is equal to "any":
        Return true.
    Inspect v:
        When JNull:
            Return kind is equal to "opttext" or kind is equal to "optint".
        When JBool (b):
            Return kind is equal to "bool".
        When JNum (n):
            Return kind is equal to "int" or kind is equal to "optint".
        When JDec (m, sc):
            Return false.
        When JStr (s):
            Return kind is equal to "text" or kind is equal to "opttext".
        When JArr (elems):
            If kind is equal to "array":
                Return true.
            If kind is equal to "texts":
                Return allStrings(elems).
            Return false.
        When JObj (entries):
            Return kind is equal to "object".
    Return false.

## Note
The first way `v` breaks the closed shape `specs`, or `""` when it has exactly that
shape: an object, carrying every specified field with its kind, and nothing else.

## To shapeProblem (v: Json, specs: Seq of Text) -> Text:
    Let mutable names be a new Seq of Text.
    Repeat for spec in specs:
        Push fieldName(spec) to names.
    Inspect jsonNoExtraKeys(v, names):
        When Err (e):
            Return e.
        When Ok (fine):
            Let ignored be fine.
    Repeat for spec in specs:
        Let name be fieldName(spec).
        Let kind be fieldKind(spec).
        If jsonHas(v, name) is equal to false:
            Return "missing field \"{name}\"".
        If hasKind(jsonGet(v, name), kind) is equal to false:
            Return "field \"{name}\" is not {kind}".
    Return "".

## Note
The same check for an OPEN shape: the specified fields must be there with their
kinds, and other fields are allowed. Foreign documents grow fields between releases
(the OPDP README asks importers to tolerate them), so they are read open.

## To openShapeProblem (v: Json, specs: Seq of Text) -> Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JObj (entries):
            Let ignored be entries.
        Otherwise:
            Return "expected a JSON object".
    Repeat for spec in specs:
        Let name be fieldName(spec).
        Let kind be fieldKind(spec).
        If jsonHas(v, name) is equal to false:
            Return "missing field \"{name}\"".
        If hasKind(jsonGet(v, name), kind) is equal to false:
            Return "field \"{name}\" is not {kind}".
    Return "".

## Note
The text under `key`, or `""` when it is absent or not a string. Total: call it after
a shape check has established the field.

## To textAt (v: Json, key: Text) -> Text:
    Inspect jsonGet(v, key):
        When JStr (s):
            Return s.
        Otherwise:
            Return "".
    Return "".

## Note
The integer under `key`, or `0` when it is absent or not an integer.

## To intAt (v: Json, key: Text) -> Int:
    Inspect jsonGet(v, key):
        When JNum (n):
            Return n.
        Otherwise:
            Return 0.
    Return 0.

## Note
The boolean under `key`, or `false` when it is absent or not a boolean.

## To boolAt (v: Json, key: Text) -> Bool:
    Inspect jsonGet(v, key):
        When JBool (b):
            Return b.
        Otherwise:
            Return false.
    Return false.

## Note
The strings of the array under `key`, in order; non-strings are skipped and an absent
field is the empty list.

## To textsAt (v: Json, key: Text) -> Seq of Text:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Text.
    Inspect jsonGet(v, key):
        When JArr (elems):
            Repeat for e in elems:
                Inspect e:
                    When JStr (s):
                        Push s to out.
                    Otherwise:
                        Let ignored be e.
        Otherwise:
            Return out.
    Return out.

## Note
The text under `key` when it is a string, `none` when it is `null` or absent. This is
how the factory keeps a missing value apart from an empty one.

## To optTextAt (v: Json, key: Text) -> Option of Text:
    Inspect jsonGet(v, key):
        When JStr (s):
            Return some s.
        Otherwise:
            Return none.
    Return none.

## Note
The value at a dotted path through nested objects (`"tractability.score"`), or
`JNull` when any step is missing.

## To jsonPath (v: Json, path: Text) -> Json:
    Let mutable here be v.
    Repeat for step in split(path, "."):
        Set here to jsonGet(here, step).
    Return here.

## Note
Ten to the power `k`, for `k` at least zero.

## To tenTo (k: Int) -> Int:
    Let mutable out be 1.
    Repeat for i from 1 to k:
        Set out to out * 10.
    Return out.

## Note
A JSON number as an exact integer count of `10^-digits` units: `5.4` at two digits is
`540`, `0.25` at four digits is `2500`, `7` at two digits is `700`.

The atlas stores its scores as short decimals and the factory computes on integers,
so this is the one door between them. It is EXACT: a number with more decimals
than `digits` is refused rather than rounded, because a rank computed from a
silently rounded input is a rank nobody can reproduce from the source.

## To jsonScaled (v: Json, digits: Int) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JNum (n):
            Return a new Ok with value (n * tenTo(digits)).
        When JDec (m, sc):
            Let places be 0 - sc.
            If places is greater than digits:
                Return a new Err with error "{jsonSerialize(v)} has more than {digits} decimals".
            Return a new Ok with value (m * tenTo(digits - places)).
        Otherwise:
            Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON number, found {jsonSerialize(v)}".
    Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON number".

## Note
A non-negative JSON number as a count of `10^-digits` units, rounded UP when it is
finer than that: `0.0000012` at six digits is `2`. This is the door for money
coming IN — a provider's reported cost, a price per token — where rounding down
would let a budget be overspent one fraction of a micro-dollar at a time, and
rounding up only ever keeps a little in reserve.

## To jsonScaledCeil (v: Json, digits: Int) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Inspect v:
        When JNum (n):
            If n is less than 0:
                Return a new Err with error "{n} is negative".
            Return a new Ok with value (n * tenTo(digits)).
        When JDec (m, sc):
            If m is less than 0:
                Return a new Err with error "{jsonSerialize(v)} is negative".
            Let places be 0 - sc.
            If places is at most digits:
                Return a new Ok with value (m * tenTo(digits - places)).
            Let unit be tenTo(places - digits).
            Let mutable q be m / unit.
            If m % unit is greater than 0:
                Set q to q + 1.
            Return a new Ok with value q.
        Otherwise:
            Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON number, found {jsonSerialize(v)}".
    Return a new Err with error "expected a JSON number".

## Note
A decimal written as TEXT (`"0.0000025"`, `"2.5e-06"`) — the way OpenRouter
publishes its prices — as a count of `10^-digits` units, rounded up. The text is
read by the JSON number grammar, so exactly the spellings JSON allows are allowed.

## To decimalTextScaledCeil (t: Text, digits: Int) -> Result of Int and Text:
    Inspect jsonTryParse(trim(t)):
        When JOk (v):
            Inspect v:
                When JNum (n):
                    Return jsonScaledCeil(v, digits).
                When JDec (m, sc):
                    Return jsonScaledCeil(v, digits).
                Otherwise:
                    Return a new Err with error "\"{t}\" is not a number".
        When JErr (e):
            Return a new Err with error "\"{t}\" is not a number".
    Return a new Err with error "\"{t}\" is not a number".

## Note
An arbitrary integer folded onto `0 … size - 1`, so a property test can range over
every integer and still speak about amounts that cannot be negative.

## To foldTo (n: Int, size: Int) -> Int:
    Let m be n % size.
    If m is less than 0:
        Return m + size.
    Return m.

## Note
Parse a document strictly (a duplicated key is an error, never a silent overwrite)
into a `Result`, so callers compose it with the rest of their decoding.

## To parseStrict (text: Text) -> Result of Json and Text:
    Inspect jsonParseStrict(text):
        When JOk (v):
            Return a new Ok with value v.
        When JErr (m):
            Return a new Err with error m.
    Return a new Err with error "unparsed".

## Test: a field specification splits into its name and kind

Expect that fieldName("seq:int") is equal to "seq".
Expect that fieldKind("seq:int") is equal to "int".
Expect that fieldKind("payload") is equal to "any".

## Test: a closed shape accepts exactly its fields

Let v be jsonParse("{{\"a\": \"x\", \"n\": 3, \"ok\": true, \"tags\": [\"p\", \"q\"], \"m\": null}}").
Expect that shapeProblem(v, ["a:text", "n:int", "ok:bool", "tags:texts", "m:opttext"]) is equal to "".

## Test: a closed shape names the first thing wrong with a document

Let v be jsonParse("{{\"a\": \"x\", \"n\": \"3\"}}").
Expect that shapeProblem(v, ["a:text", "n:int"]) is equal to "field \"n\" is not int".
Expect that shapeProblem(v, ["a:text", "n:text", "z:int"]) is equal to "missing field \"z\"".
Expect that shapeProblem(v, ["a:text"]) is equal to "unexpected key \"n\"".
Expect that shapeProblem(jsonParse("[1]"), ["a:text"]) is equal to "expected a JSON object".

## Test: an array of strings is texts, and a mixed array is not

Let v be jsonParse("{{\"good\": [\"a\"], \"bad\": [\"a\", 1], \"none\": []}}").
Expect that hasKind(jsonGet(v, "good"), "texts") is equal to true.
Expect that hasKind(jsonGet(v, "bad"), "texts") is equal to false.
Expect that hasKind(jsonGet(v, "none"), "texts") is equal to true.
Expect that hasKind(jsonGet(v, "bad"), "array") is equal to true.

## Test: an open shape tolerates fields it does not name

Let v be jsonParse("{{\"a\": \"x\", \"extra\": 1}}").
Expect that openShapeProblem(v, ["a:text"]) is equal to "".
Expect that openShapeProblem(v, ["b:text"]) is equal to "missing field \"b\"".

## Test: total accessors read what a shape check established

Let v be jsonParse("{{\"a\": \"x\", \"n\": -4, \"ok\": true, \"tags\": [\"p\", \"q\"], \"m\": null}}").
Expect that textAt(v, "a") is equal to "x".
Expect that intAt(v, "n") is equal to -4.
Expect that boolAt(v, "ok") is equal to true.
Expect that textsAt(v, "tags") is equal to ["p", "q"].
Expect that optTextAt(v, "m") is none.
Expect that optTextAt(v, "a") is equal to some "x".
Expect that textAt(v, "missing") is equal to "".

## Test: a dotted path walks nested objects

Let v be jsonParse("{{\"t\": {{\"p\": {{\"low\": 0.4}}}}}}").
Expect that jsonSerialize(jsonPath(v, "t.p.low")) is equal to "0.4".
Expect that jsonIsNull(jsonPath(v, "t.q.low")) is equal to true.

## Test: decimals become exact scaled integers

For each of these cases:

| doc     | digits | scaled |
|---------|--------|--------|
| "5.4"   | 3      | 5400   |
| "0.25"  | 4      | 2500   |
| "7"     | 3      | 7000   |
| "0.07"  | 4      | 700    |
| "-1.5"  | 1      | -15    |

Inspect jsonScaled(jsonParse(doc), digits):
    When Ok (n):
        Expect that n is equal to scaled.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "no error".

## Test: a decimal finer than the scale is refused, not rounded

Inspect jsonScaled(jsonParse("0.125"), 2):
    When Ok (n):
        Expect that n is equal to -1.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "0.125 has more than 2 decimals".

## Test: money coming in is rounded up, never down

For each of these cases:

| doc          | digits | scaled |
|--------------|--------|--------|
| "0.0000012"  | 6      | 2      |
| "0.000001"   | 6      | 1      |
| "0.00000100" | 6      | 1      |
| "1.5"        | 6      | 1500000|
| "3"          | 6      | 3000000|
| "1e-7"       | 6      | 1      |
| "0"          | 6      | 0      |

Inspect jsonScaledCeil(jsonParse(doc), digits):
    When Ok (n):
        Expect that n is equal to scaled.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "no error".

## Test: a negative amount of money coming in is refused

Inspect jsonScaledCeil(jsonParse("-0.5"), 6):
    When Ok (n):
        Expect that n is equal to 0.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "-0.5 is negative".

## Test: a price written as text is read by the JSON number grammar

Inspect decimalTextScaledCeil("0.0000025", 12):
    When Ok (n):
        Expect that n is equal to 2500000.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "no error".
Inspect decimalTextScaledCeil("2.5e-06", 12):
    When Ok (n):
        Expect that n is equal to 2500000.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "no error".
Inspect decimalTextScaledCeil("free", 12):
    When Ok (n):
        Expect that n is equal to -1.
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e is equal to "\"free\" is not a number".

## Test: a strict parse refuses a duplicated key

Inspect parseStrict("{{\"a\": 1, \"a\": 2}}"):
    When Ok (v):
        Expect that jsonSerialize(v) is equal to "refused".
    When Err (e):
        Expect that e contains "duplicate".
